<Article slug="wood-vs-vinyl-vs-aluminum-fence">
  <p>The right fence material depends on three things: what the fence is for, how much upkeep you want, and what your city allows on your lot. Here’s how the four materials we install compare.</p>

  <h2>At a glance</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="rules-table">
      <caption>How the materials compare</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Material</th><th scope="col">Privacy</th><th scope="col">Upkeep</th><th scope="col">Up-front cost</th><th scope="col">Best for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/services/wood-fencing/">Wood</a></th><td>Full (privacy, shadow box) or none (picket, split rail)</td><td>Stain every few years</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Backyards, custom heights and styles</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/services/vinyl-fencing/">Vinyl</a></th><td>Full</td><td>Rinse occasionally</td><td>Higher</td><td>Low-maintenance privacy</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/services/aluminum-fencing/">Aluminum</a></th><td>None, open view</td><td>Minimal, doesn’t rust</td><td>Higher</td><td>Waterfront lots, front yards, pools</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/services/chain-link-fencing/">Chain link</a></th><td>None</td><td>Minimal</td><td>Lowest</td><td>Dogs, back lots, large areas</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Wood</h2>
  <p>Wood is the most flexible material. It can be cut to fit odd lot lines and slopes, built as a solid privacy fence, a two-sided <strong>shadow box</strong>, a <strong>picket</strong> fence or open <strong>split rail</strong>, and stained any color. The trade-off is upkeep: plan on a cleaning and a coat of <a href="/services/fence-staining/">stain</a> every few years to keep it from graying. In Clay Township, the ordinance requires the side facing neighbors to be "finished," for example stained or painted.</p>

  <h2>Vinyl</h2>
  <p>Vinyl gives you privacy without the maintenance. There’s no staining, painting or sealing, and most panels look the same from both sides, so you never have to argue over who gets the "good side." It costs more up front than wood.</p>

  <h2>Aluminum</h2>
  <p>Black ornamental aluminum looks like wrought iron but doesn’t rust. It’s open, so it doesn’t give privacy, and that’s exactly why it’s often the <em>only</em> option on waterfront lots. St. Clair Shores bans privacy fences on waterfront and canal lots, and Chesterfield Township allows only decorative aluminum or wrought iron (up to 48″) on the water side of Anchor Bay and Salt River lots.</p>

  <h2>Chain link</h2>
  <p>Chain link is the budget workhorse for dog yards and back property lines. It’s durable and see-through. Some ordinances don’t treat it as decorative: Clay Township excludes it from decorative front-yard fencing, and Chesterfield Township bans it from waterfront front yards.</p>

  <h2>How to decide</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Start with your lot’s rules.</strong> Waterfront, corner lot or front yard? That may decide for you. See the <a href="/resources/fence-permits-st-clair-shores-macomb-county/">local permit guide</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Decide how much privacy you need.</strong> Full screening means wood privacy, shadow box or vinyl.</li>
    <li><strong>Be honest about upkeep.</strong> If you won’t stain, choose vinyl or aluminum.</li>
    <li><strong>Get two prices.</strong> We’re happy to price wood and vinyl side by side so you can compare.</li>
  </ol>
</Article>
